spraggej@Jeff-Lab.QueensU.CA wrote:
>1) someone posts an opinion that you consider slander, or you think
>   defines them as one sick puppy (examples I have seen over the past
>   couple of months include: the Quebec education laws amount to child
>   abuse, five year olds can consent to sex with adults, and SRI did 
>   nothing wrong); each of these got someone's dander severely up.
>
>2) some user feels the temptation to send a letter to the site admin.
>   pointing out what an infantile, weird, warped individual they have
>   working for them.
>
>This raises the question: when can you level such a complaint. 

OK, let's make a "first cut" in our discussion.

I believe that facilities should be grouped into "public forums" and 
"public services."  I believe that "forum" implies a group (not just
two-way, but many-way) nature.  Therefore, here's a "first cut" at 
grouping some common facilities:

	Public Service			Public Forum
	--------------------------      --------------------------
	Terminal servers		Usenet news
	Public printers			Chat systems (irc, RELAY, etc.)
	Public labs 			Most email mailing lists
	Some email mailing lists	Anonymous FTP with upload  
	Individual email facilities
	Server diskspace
	Anonymous FTP w/o uploads 

I submit that the "services" and "forums" deserve (perhaps even require)
a different approach when handling complaints.  Some facilities may fall 
into both categories; an invitation-only or moderated mailing list may be
a hybrid animal, part "service" and part "forum."  You'll notice that I've
listed variants of FTP in both categories.  Some FTP sites allow uploading
of files, while others are "read only;" I consider that an important dis-
tinction when handling complaints.

Now, then, where do these differences come into play?

Let's take a common example and "make it fit" different facilities.
Consider the typical "obscene material" problem.  Suppose that some
user has some material which others find offensive or obscene.  I
would suggest that the means of presentation/distribution make a *big*
difference in the perspective from which complaints should be handled.
Here's a few quick thoughts on various media:

	- Material is posted to Usenet, in an appropriate group (say,
	  alt.sex.bondage). 
	  Result: "public forum"....protected speech....tend to
		  dismiss ordinary complaint.
	- Material is sent to unrelated mailing list (say, NOVELL-L).
	  Result: minor problem.....starting to exceed boundaries of
		  limited public forum doctrine.....tend to take *very*
		  minor action ("have a chat" with the user, perhaps).
	- Material is broadcast to screens in public lab.
	  Result: disruption of "public service".....not protected speech.
		  ("time, place and manner" restrictions)  Some action should 
		  be taken; again, "have a chat" with the user.)
	- Material is broadcast via .plan file (shown by finger command)
	  Result: A rather dicey case; while .plan files have not tradi-
		  tionally served as a "public forum," they are often used
		  for personal expression.  Direct complainant to take mat-
		  ter up with individual user; no need/reason for admin
		  involvement.
	- Material is broadcast on IRC channel(s)
	  Result: I don't run an IRC server.  Additionally, IRC serves as
		  a limited public forum to some degree.  Suggest that mat-
		  ter be handled by IRC admins; it isn't my call, because
		  it's *their* forum.
	- Material is made available via email server or server disk space
	  Result: "public service," but also rather private usage; others
		  can only get this stuff by taking deliberate/voluntary
		  steps.  No need/reason for discipline, but I can't jus-
		  tify giving more resources for nonacademic use like this.
	- Material is mass-mailed to a gazillion people
	  Result: Individual email is a "public service."  Mass-mailing 
	          unrequested material (of *any* sort) is an abuse of that
		  service.  Time to "have a chat" with the responsible party.
	- Material is uploaded to FTP archive
	  Result: Hey, it's *their* site.  Suggest that complainant get
		  in touch with user directly.  If user ignores requests
		  from FTP site admins, then I may reinforce their request
		  as a directive from local staff.

In each case, we must consider the means of presentation, not necessarily
the material itself.  

You'll notice that none of my scenarios resulted in disciplinary action.
Personally, I've been a university admin for 4 years without filing char-
ges; I believe that they should be a last resort.  The overwhelming ma-
jority of our local problems have been resolved in 15 minutes or less. 
That includes sharing passwords, cracking software, and forged email;
I've never had a "repeat offender.  8)

--Wes

