Agreed; however, an admin is rarely in a position to take such drastic
action.  I'd also suggest that we can't really compare a personal self-
defense situation (real or imagined) with the virtual defense of one's
computing systems.

Of course, the initial tone of the complaint sets the tone for the
entire situation.  My notes, which read as "hey, you know, this is a
no-no; don't do it any more, ok?", seem to set a calm tone; I have,
however, received notes like "hey, this guys is probing our entire
network service structure -- rein him in!"  You just have to stay
calm -- vigilance doesn't have to imply paranoia or frenzy.

I would think that the inclusion of other admins in the situation (such 
as I and the admin of some remote site whose user is doing something flaky 
at engr.uky.edu) would tend to *soften* the reactions of all involved.  
You aren't going to find *that* many hotheads.  I've only had to "cool
down" another admin on one occasion; he was ready to nail the user to the
wall for sharing userids on my system, and I simply said that I would not
support those charges in a first offense.   Without my support, the char-
ges don't fly.

--Wes


