Date: 9 Aug 1993 22:28:38 GMT
Message-ID: <246j2m$h6j@news.bu.edu>



Hey Gang!

I thought y'all might be interedted in an editorial I read in this week's 
_Chronicle of Higher Education_. It's called "Overcoming Legal Barriers to
Regulating Hate Speech on Campus", by Richard Delgado, author of _Words that
Wound: Critical Race Theory, Assaultive Speech, and the First Amendment_,
and his research assistant, Jean Stefancic.

This dynamic duo begin by bewailing the difficulty of righteous censoring in
America:

           "A few years ago, when feminists in Indianapolis and
            Minneapolis succeeded in enacting antipornography
            ordinances (drafted by the writer-activist Andrea
            Dworkin and the University of Michigan law professor
            Catharine MacKinnon), courts and mayors struck them
            down. Later, two federaL courts struck down hate-
            speech codes at the Universities of Wisconsin [which
            Delgado helped draft--no vested interest here!] and
            Michigan."

They go on: "the judiciary remains conservative [?!], and much of the public
harbors little sympathy for feminist or minority group causes." I assume
they are talking about the same public that made _Backlash_ a national
bestseller. As regard the judiciary, they obviously take the view that 
freedom of speech is conservative (we'll see this in spades later).

Now, you can't advocate censorship in a (somewhat) free society without
justification, however tenuous. Here it is:

            "We [who?] are beginning to recognize that hateful
             speech is not the symptom of subordination (as it is
             often thought to be), but its very source.

                           [...]

              We are beginning to understand how society uses
              such [racist and sexist] images to construct a social
              reality in which women and blacks are always at risk,
              one in which each new slight or injury reverberates 
              against a history of similar ones. After years of
              repitition, offenses [...] gain a power that most
              insults directed at individuals lack - the power to
              demoralize.

              Words can create catagories, expectations and subconcious
              assumptions so deep-seated that only troublesome
              mechanisms such as affirmative action can make headway
              against the system of subordination they support. [...]
              [G]ains for one group may well come at the expense of
              another [like that group that likes to speak their minds?] [.]

This is the language-creates-reality school of thought. They may have a point.
Certainly what we think about people is going to be mediated by stereotypes, but 
mainly it's an excuse for the fact that you haven't impressed people who are
impressed by achievement. It's the graduate student's version of "I got a 'D'
because my teacher hates me,".

What can be done about this? Our heroes have a solution: "...defining a 
radically new understanding of a First Amendment that protects equality
as much as it has liberty..." Funny, I don't recall the First Amendment
making any reference to equality whatsoever. Still, the priceless pair
admit that this will take some doing:

               "Longer term solutions and broader remedies will
                entail at least the following tasks:

                -Lawyers [of course] and social scientists must be
                prepared to demonstrate through research the harm done 
                by racial insults and pornography [in fact this has 
                been tried, most notably by the Meese commission,
                without much success] and to show how the law's
                conception of harm can be reasonably [!] expanded
                to include notions of damaged identity.  "

There are three more points in a similar vein.

                      ____________________________

Can these people possibly be serious? A freedom of speech that recognises
only equality is no freedom at all! Only *ABSOLUTE* freedom is freedom.
once freedom is restricted, control follows. That's fine if your group is
in control, as our authors' group would be under the equality principle,
but suppose we were under the fashion principle and only those with the 
latest Milan suit were allowed to speak. That's a silly examplem of course,
but the principle holds. Once you set up a system where there are approved 
opinions ("equality is the most important thing"), you've opened up the door,
and it's only a matter of time until the cops are marching through it.

Thanks for reading. Vote Libertarian.

--Mista Kotta

