Ignorance represents about the weakest shield you can imagine. Remember,
what people don't know, they can let alone, or they can demonize. I would
much rather have the public know as much as they can about us (remember, 
at least one in 250 North Americans now has some connection to the net,
and I suspect I should put the proportion higher).

And if they can appeal to us to behave responsibly out of knowledge of what
we do and what we offer, so much the better. The idea of the net as an
(expensive) sandbox for an enlightened (privileged) few does not appeal to
me. 

> "We" aren't communicating about the net with the rest of the public,
> in general. Joe Abernathy is an exception-- and you see the treatment
> he gets from the rest of the net.  Any censorship will be driven by an
> authority-type, not the public.  They will bring their case to the
> public if they feel it will increase their ability to censor, but it
> is they who will be the driving force, not the public.  This is true
> for censorship in general-- it is always groups associated with
> authority (e.g. the PMRC) who advocate the censorship.  It is in fact
> true for restrictive legislation in general-- witness the "NIMBY"
> committees which appear in many areas to object to one thing or
> another.  They are always composed of the same people, no matter what
> the cause.  And those people are usually authorities or (often)
> relatives of authorities.

1) everything we do ultimately communicates with the "public" (to whom
   we also belong).

2) I have never heard of Joe Abernathy.

3) I don't accept the term "the rest of the net" as a reference to a
   coherent entity who can give "treatment" to anyone.

4) You keep referring to "an authority type". That characterisation does
   not usefully refer to anyone. Most people reject at least some types
   of authority; almost all of us embrace some type of authority (even
   if only the "authority" that comes fro moral suation, or the willingness
   to dissassociate yourself from a particular person). 

5) "the same people" -- give me a break! The same people don't run 
   anti-imigration ads that march in anti-pornography marches. The
   same people don't always march against pornography and racism. 

6) "relatives" -- if you really mean to suggest that the impulse gets
   passed along with the genes, you should get professional help. If you
   just meant a dig at poor Tipper Gore, give it up; the days when a woman
   had to get a lobotomy before marrying a politician have ended, thank
   goodness.

> Nope.  Each time they are attacking a target on the very edge of
> acceptability-- and since each victory narrows the bounds of
> acceptability, they will always have a new target.

The presence or absence of a rule doesn't determine people's attitudes. I 
don't need a rule to make child pornography unacceptable to me. If a rule
on the net provides for remedies in the case of child porn, that won't make
other things either more or less "acceptable" to me. Try to deal with 
something I don't accept, and I'll support you. Try to get rid of something
I do accept, and I'll fight you.

The debate defines the "edge of acceptability", and no "authorities" can
really keep something illegal if the majority of the people defend it.

> That's fine as to the posting part, as it isn't censorship but
> prosecution of individual crimes.  Downloading, though... you ought to
> take another look at the implications of that.

I said "illegally". Since Canadian law now prohibits knowingly posessing
child pornography, knowingly committing an act that will cause it to come
into you posession (on disk, screen, paper) involves a violation of the
criminal code here. I see no reason why the police should bust someone for
bringing a magazine across the border in a car, but not for pulling a JPEG
across a telephone line.

> That might be CYA-- regrettable, but necessary.  Though IMO it might
> expose the administrator much MORE if he misses something, because now
> the cops know he looked at it all.

I despise CYA. Try TRTTD (the right thing to do) instead. 

> Now THAT is blacklisting, and about as objectionable as you can get.

So do you demand that the sysadmins disable the kill facility from your
news reader? Or do you accept the right of individuals to blacklist material
that offends them, but deny the right of a site to refuse disk space to a
poster who refuses to respect their local rules and laws?

[ on child pornography ]

> Assuming, for the sake of argument, that it does provide positive reinforcement:
> 
> Positive reinforcement of crimes is not in itself a crime.  I can go
> out and write stories, complete with graphic illustrations, of the
> worst kinds of murder, rape, and torture, and portray the criminals as
> heroes in the stories.  I can cast the victims as loving it, and I can
> even take photographs of sexual acts which match the descriptions of
> the rapes in my story, with the victims appearing to love it.   And
> this all can be 100% protected free speech, in the United States, at
> least.

Neither my site, nor the site mainly involved in this issue fall inside the
United States. Here, those pictures of the "worst kind of torture" will
draw you a visit from the police, followed (probably) by charges.

I don't know about the US Code. I do know that in Canada, an offence exists
called "counselling to an offence", and serious encouragement of child
pornography skirts it. 

> Telling a pedophile there are other pedophiles out there is not
> censorable speech, nor should it be.

But if it encourages the pedophile to go out and rape children, what
then? Do you just say that yes, you did condone pedophilia, and yes,
you did support it, but you take no responsibility for the result?

Frankly, if the latter, I really have no major problem with the idea of
a daemon on the server looking for your userids and erasing any of your 
posts (assuming you posted material supporting child rape, and we asked
you to keep it off our disk, and you refused).

If that makes me one of "them", fine. I could look at the net, say "prodigy",
and sleep at night. I couldn't do that if I thought some libertarian
squeamishness on my part had caused me to failt to prevent the death of a
child.

