>we shouldn't articulate (or debate) any limits at all?

Actually, I have been arguing against the idea that it is possible to
prevent future attempts to censor the net by using moral pressure to
discourage the type of irresponsible postings that lead people to try
to censor the net.  Since you say that is not what you were claiming we
need no longer argue about it.

But there is an additional point I've been wanting to make but could
never find the opportunity to argue for:  That preventing censorship
such as Prevost-Derbecker wants (ie. removal of a newsgroup because of
a minority of postings that are offensive) can only be done by opposing
the would-be censors, not the members of the net who posted the
articles that offended the would-be censors.  Again, since this is not
contrary to what you've been saying, we need not argue about it.

Ignoring any possible hidden agenda Prevost-Derbecker might have, the
logic of her expressed agenda threatens more than just the sex and porn
newsgroups.

>Look, like Ms. Prevost-Derbecker or not, she has come out with a rational
>argument. She claims that certain material on the net will cause harm, and that
>therefore the university authorities must delete this material from the
>Waterloo net. She may have a few weaknesses on the technical side, but her
>logic makes perfect sense.

Prevost-Derbecker's argument seems to be that the newsgroup that
carries harmful/illegal material must be removed even though that
material is a minority of the contents of the group, and even though
most of the content of the group is unobjectionable or even
praiseworthy.  As she said to the KW Record, if we eliminate erotica
too, so be it.  If UW were to agree to this thinking and put it into
practice, they would have no good reason not to remove
talk.politics.misc.  A significant fraction of that group is hate
literature, which is both harmful and illegal here.  And I have seen
anti-semitism and holocaust revisionism in alt.censorship...

Prevost-Derbecker did not mention any kind of harm to the KW Record
other than harassment.  I can't comment on possible implications of
that until I get a better idea of what she considers to be harassment,
and how she thinks that harassment results from Usenet porn.

>I have yet to see anybody who wants to support the "freedom" of the net say 
>either: child pornography causes harm, so we shouldn't have it, and we can
>get rid of it without demolishing alt.sex.* or any of the other newsgroups; 
>or: child pornography does not really do anyone any harm, because...
>Either statement would honestly address the issues Ms. Prevost-Derbecker
>has raised. 

If the harm you refer to here is the encouragement of child rape you've
mentioned in other articles, as far as I can tell from the KW Record
this is not Prevost-Derbecker's argument, but yours.  When I get a copy
of the Women's Centre's statement, perhaps I'll find out otherwise, but
as I see it the responses you are demanding are not responses to
Prevost-Derbecker but to you.

And I can't honestly agree with either of the two positions you claim I
must agree with.

I'll grant that the distribution of some child pornography will
probably cause harm by encouraging some people who would not otherwise
have done so to molest children.  I expect that it would not be many
people.  I don't know if any of the people so affected will have been
affected by Usenet porn, but the danger is there.

I do not believe that the posting of child porn or its arrival on UW's
computers can be prevented without removing the newsgroups it appears
in.  At best, it is possible to detect and remove child porn, but at
the time of its removal most of it will have been on our system for 6
or more hours.  Most people who wanted to would have had a chance to
view it, and it would have been passed along to sites we feed news to.

So, it's dangerous, and we can't stop it without removing a.b.p.e.
Nevertheless, I would oppose the removal of a.b.p.e if it were done for
these reasons.  In any newsgroup where people get to post political
opinions there will be postings of ideas that are harmful or
dangerous.  Just as we can't absolutely prevent child porn in a.b.p.e,
we can't prevent neo-nazism and homophia in talk.politics.misc,
misogyny and misandry in soc.men and soc.women, advocacy for theocracy
in talk.religion.misc, etc.  The argument that a.b.p.e should be
removed implies that these other groups should be removed too.

It is their uncontrollability that makes these newsgroups prone to
removal based on the same arguments as for a.b.p.e.  It is their
uncontrollability that I value.  Your arguments attack exactly the
thing that I value most about Usenet.  The implications of your
arguments would forbid free political speech on the net.

>In the case of child pornography, I have made quite clear where I think
>the harm lies. I have yet to get a single answer to those points.

You have my response.  I might be induced to clarify it.

>First off, I have never played devil's advocate here. If you want to know, 
>my position stands thus:
>
>1) I think the first line of defence for a free community like this
>   comes from a sense of responsibility. In the face of an expression
>   of concern such as the current one, we have an obligation to either
>   defend the material (in the name of free speech), or help find a way
>   to prevent it from doing harm. 

There's a fine distinction I'd like to make here.  I can be opposed to
the reason given for removing a.b.p.e without being opposed to removing
a.b.p.e.  If the reason given for removing a.b.p.e implies that
political newsgroups must be removed, then I will disagree.

>2) Ideally, we could come to a general consensus (based on reasonable
>   principles) about what we can accept on the news groups. When people
>   post stuff that violates these principles, we should first e-mail 
>   them, asking them either not to post the material in the future, or
>   to restrict it to domains that do accept it.
>
>3) I think a site has two legitimate defences against an individual who
>   persistently refuses to consider the concerns expressed: in the case 
>   of an individual whose posts violate their own laws as well as the
>   laws of the receiving site, a complaint to their system administrators
>   would make sense; I think a site can also take measures to block all
>   the material from a specific user at another site, if that user has
>   posted obnoxious or illegal material and refused all requests to stop. 

One little point.  Complaining to the sysadmin, and blocking articles
from a specific user *is* enforcement.  Perhaps you should stop denying
that you intend enforcement.
