emv@garnet.msen.com (Edward Vielmetti) wrote:
>This is also clearly irrelevant.  The case in question involved
>e-mail that included threats of explicitly sexual violence.  
>It was not the first case of this sort of thing happening, and
>it will not be the last.
>
>Given that sort of shit on the nets putting people on edge I
>would not be at all hesitant to treat some yo-yo sending out
>things that are "offensive" and "sexual" unsolicited to other
>users as worthy of contempt.  

True, but I'd submit that there is a *huge* difference between
email and a forum like Usenet.  Usenet is a "one-to-many" en-
vironment, while most email is "one-to-one."  If we believe
that harassment is an individual-to-individual situation (as
I believe), it can be difficult to apply its precepts to public 
forums.

If a person is sending harassing email directly to you, I can
easily classify it harassment, since it was directed specifi-
cally at you.  I can't quite accept a claim of "harassment" 
which results from voluntary viewing of publicly placed material.   
(Of course, I'm assuming that the material is placed in the
proper forum.  Someone who throws explicit material in, say,
comp.sys.att should be dealt with under the "off topic" issue.)
I find it interesting that I've never heard of a "harassment" 
claim as a result of an article/pictorial in newsmagazines or 
the local paper, even though the local paper carries ads for 
topless bars and "massage services."  Why should an electronic 
forum be treated differently?

Can a general Usenet posting constitute:
Slander?     Perhaps.
Defamation?  You never know.  
Offense?     Quite frequently.  
Harassment?  Unless directed at a specific person, that's a 
             *very* dicey call in a public forum.

In the case of adult/explicit material, I should think that many 
who claim to be harassed by its *presence* are actually expressing
offense.  That's a big difference, and it should be noted as such.
Of course, the *presentation* of such material may be harassment,
if it is directed at a particular person; the commonly used exam-
ple of "popping a GIF onto another person's screen" could certainly
be a valid case of harassment.  However, I can't accept the notion
that the mere existence of the material constitutes harassment.  If
we accept that notion, quite a few libraries are in for some work,
as they cleanse their holdings of all that "harassing" material.


--Wes

