From caf-talk Caf Aug 10 20:22:13 1993
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.censorship
Subject: "Intentional infliction of emotional harm"
Date: 10 Aug 1993 20:21:38 -0400
Message-ID: <249e2i$i0i@eff.org>

Is it legal to repeatedly insult or ridicule a person in a open forum
such as Netnews? What if the person isn't even on the Net and so can't
respond? Here is what two books have to say about the right to (a type
of) privacy and personal-injury claims based on alledged intentional
infliction of emotional harm.

[Disclaimer: I'm not a lawyer. This is all U.S.-centered.  I do not
represent EFF; this is just me.]

- Carl

=======================================
[_Law of the Student Press_ by the Student Press Law Center, 1985]

"Basically there are four kinds of invasion of privacy:
  I. Intrusion: unreasonable intruding, usually physically, upon the
solitude or seclusion of another. this type of privacy invasion occurs
when an overzealous report uses some type of eavesdropping device,
tape recorder, or camera to record a person's private life without his
knowledge or permission. It also occurs by misrepresenting oneself to
gain access to a private person or place or by trespasing upon private
property."  [...]

II. Appropriation: the unauthorized use of a person's name,
photography, likeness, or endorsement to promote the sale of a
commercial produce.  [...]

III. False light: publishing material which creates a false image of
an individual, i.e. making the person appear to be something he is
not. This type of invasion of privacy occurs when a paper carries a
photo with an incorrect or misleading caption.  [...]

IV. Publication of private and embarrassing facts: this is by far the
most troublesome and most important type of invasion of privacy. A
number of courts now recognize a right to sue for the publication of
offensive and objectional information about an individual who is
_private_ and _not newsworthy_. Unlike libel, the publication is
actual even if the information is true.

In order to be successful in such a privacy suit, it must be show that
the facts were _private_, ie. not already in the public domain. The
only such suite to reach the Supreme Court graphically illustrates this
point. In _Cox Broadcasting Co. v. Cohn [56], an Atlanta television
station was sued after it broadcast the name of a 17-year-old rape
victim. The Court dismissed the suit on the ground that the information
was not private since it was obtained at the trail which was open to
the public.
[...]

========================================== 
[_The Rights of Authors, Artists, and Other Creative People_ by
Kenneth P. Norwick and Jerry Simon Chasen, 1992 (An American Civil
Liberties Union Handbook:]

[p185...]
[Q:] What is a _private-facts_ invasion of privacy?
[A:] It has been defined as follows:

One who gives publicity to a matter concerning the private life of
another is subject to liability to the other for invasion of his
privacy if the matter publicized is of a kind that:
 (a) would be highly offensive to a reasonable person
 (b) is not of legitimate concern to the public [191]

[...]

[Q:] What other kinds of personal-injury claims can be asserted
against creative people?  [A:] Although most of the claims assert
either libel or invasion of privacy, and often both, other kinds of
claims can be bought. The recent case of _Falwell v. Hustler Magazine_
provides a good example of one such category. _Hustler_ magazine
published an ad parody that portrayed the Reverend Jerry Falwell as
having had sex with his mother in an outhouse and getting drunk before
preaching, among similar charges. The trail judge dismissed Falwell's
privacy claims before trial, and the jury rejected Falwell's privacy
claims, find that the parody constituted the "intentional infliction
of emotional harm" and awarded him $200,00 in damages.

A unanimous Supreme Court in 1988 overturned Falwell's victory,
holding that the contents of the ad parody could not reasonable be
understood as stating facts about Falwell and that therefore the
First Amendment precluded recover, despite the defendant's intention
to cause the plaintiff pain and distress. But the Court did leave
open, however slightly, the viability of such claims in the future,
stating that such plaintiff could recover for the intentional
infliction of emotional distress when they can show in addition "that
the publication contains a false statement of fact which was made with
'actual malice'".
[...]

7. _Restatement (Second) of Torts $$559 (hereinafter,
_Restatement)(1977).  This "model" legal code, prepared by legal
scholars, has been extremely influential in the development of libel
law.
[...]
