Depends on what you mean by "opposing". If by "opposing" you mean a statement
such as: removing whole groups involves a gross over-reaction to the problem,
and it won't keep the pornography you have expressed concern about over our
system any way, then I oppose Ms. Prevost-Derbecker, and I certainly think
of this type of "opposition" as a way to support the freedom of the net.

If by "opposing" you mean rejecting out of hand anything she might say,
demanding the "freedom" to post and download exactly what we want with
zero regard for the rights of anyone else; then I don't consider that
either responsible opposition to Ms. Prevost-Derbecker, nor do I think
it will meaningfully uphold the freedom of the net.

> Ignoring any possible hidden agenda Prevost-Derbecker might have, the
> logic of her expressed agenda threatens more than just the sex and porn
> newsgroups.

I'd question that. We know that she has shown a very shaky grasp of the
technical side of the net; I have to wonder if she really understands how
newsgroups really work. She may not understand that you can delete illegal
and/or disallowed material without deleting the whole group. I suspect from
what I have seen of her contribution to the debate that she may not have any
idea how we can and can't block material. 

> Prevost-Derbecker's argument seems to be that the newsgroup that
> carries harmful/illegal material must be removed even though that
> material is a minority of the contents of the group, and even though
> most of the content of the group is unobjectionable or even
> praiseworthy.  As she said to the KW Record, if we eliminate erotica
> too, so be it.  If UW were to agree to this thinking and put it into
> practice, they would have no good reason not to remove
> talk.politics.misc.  A significant fraction of that group is hate
> literature, which is both harmful and illegal here.  And I have seen
> anti-semitism and holocaust revisionism in alt.censorship...

I can't speak for her. My own response to that would come down to this:
people can post whatever they want in any unmoderated group. This may
include objectionable material in any number of contexts. But a.b.p.e and
the bondage groups exist only for posting nudity/sex, bondage fantasies, 
and so on. If you find such material to have zero or negative social value,
(I don't) you might find banning the whole group a logical response to the
presence of illegal material.

I myself have never advocated banning any news group. I consider such bans an
exercise in futility. 

> If the harm you refer to here is the encouragement of child rape you've
> mentioned in other articles, as far as I can tell from the KW Record
> this is not Prevost-Derbecker's argument, but yours.  When I get a copy
> of the Women's Centre's statement, perhaps I'll find out otherwise, but
> as I see it the responses you are demanding are not responses to
> Prevost-Derbecker but to you.

From the original KW Record quote:

      On Monday, Prevost-Derbecker said she found university newsgroups
      over the weekend that included a picture of a boy engaged in oral sex
      with a donkey and a woman with a dog.  There were other examples of
      child pornography, she said.

> And I can't honestly agree with either of the two positions you claim I
> must agree with.

I don't claim anybody "must agree" with anything. I do claim people must
address this issue (the effect of the putative presence of child pornography
in the newsgroups) if they want to claim to have addressed Ms. Prevost-Derbecker's
argument.

[ comments on child porn deleted ]

> So, it's dangerous, and we can't stop it without removing a.b.p.e.

I don't agree with removing a.b.p.e, for three reasons:

1) only a tiny minority of all posters post this stuff. Most of
   them don't want huge barrages of requests not to do it. I
   think many people who post for the "glory" will keep serious
   garbage off the server if the people at the affected site ask 
   them to.

2) for the remainder (maybe ten incidents in a bad year), the system 
   administrators can blacklist the userids that post this stuff and 
   won't respect requests not to do it.

3) which makes more sense than removing groups, since if a group gets
   removed at one site, the material that group contained will start 
   percolating through other newsgroups.

> Nevertheless, I would oppose the removal of a.b.p.e if it were done for
> these reasons.  In any newsgroup where people get to post political
> opinions there will be postings of ideas that are harmful or
> dangerous.  Just as we can't absolutely prevent child porn in a.b.p.e,
> we can't prevent neo-nazism and homophia in talk.politics.misc,
> misogyny and misandry in soc.men and soc.women, advocacy for theocracy
> in talk.religion.misc, etc.  The argument that a.b.p.e should be
> removed implies that these other groups should be removed too.

I don't think of child pornography as a political opinion. If someone posts
an article advocating sex with children, I may upbraid them for it, but I
won't call for a ban. But a picture of a child engaged in sex implied that
someone violated a child to do it. It also clearly indicates support for a
specific form of violence (child rape).

If someone posts neo-nazi ideas, I will argue with them. If someone posts
an article calling on people to kill Jews or Gypsies or Serbs or Muslims,
I'll get very nervous. If they include adresses of people they have in mind,
I'd probably call the police in the community where the people involved live.
And if they submitted a photograph of themselves with a pile of corpses, I
would certainly call the police. 

> It is their uncontrollability that makes these newsgroups prone to
> removal based on the same arguments as for a.b.p.e.  It is their
> uncontrollability that I value.  Your arguments attack exactly the
> thing that I value most about Usenet.  The implications of your
> arguments would forbid free political speech on the net.

As I have said above, I don't agree. I would never support a ban on political
speech. I wouldn't even ban an argument in favour of pedophilia. I would come
down on the distribution of material that someone raped a child to make. I 
wouldn't do it by banning newsgroups; I'd do it by sanctioning posters who
won't respect the law.

> One little point.  Complaining to the sysadmin, and blocking articles
> from a specific user *is* enforcement.  Perhaps you should stop denying
> that you intend enforcement.

Ok. As I have said, I don't consider the net a law-free zone; if laws exist,
I think we on the net should answer to them, and I can't think of any concept
of law or morality that wouldn't forbid child pornography. 

So yes, I support enforcement (if persuation fails).

Please note, however, that I don't accept the equation of enforcement of
a site's own standards and legal responsibilities with censorship.

