From: betsys@cs.umb.edu (Elizabeth Schwartz)
Subject: The other side of the coin.
Message-ID: <BETSYS.93Aug10194710@eris.cs.umb.edu>
Date: Tue, 10 Aug 1993 23:47:13 GMT


I have a couple of users who consistently draw, from the net, requests
that their accounts be cut off or threats of mail bombing. One user is
running a political mailing list and making massive posts to
soc.culture.india; another is making consistently obscene posts to
rec.humor.d and other groups.  A third user was drawing a LOT of heat
in misc.invest.technical for a while, but he listened to requests from
the net to post to more appropriate groups and to a request (or
command if you prefer) from me that he adhere to the NSFnet guidelines
on commercial use of the net, and that died down.


We've consistently defended their rights to post and to free speech,
even though the speech isn't always popular. We are bound by NSFnet
policy, NEARnet policy, Usenet guidelines, and University of
Massachusetts policy; we don't add much locally except for resource
use guidelines. 
  
  I've also had to defend, to professors, why we allow games on the
system and why users are playing games at all. I've talked to
professors about how to tell whether a game is actually using lots of
resources, and I've defended the learning value of allowing people to
explore, and the training value of letting the student operators
install X games. I don't believe in restricting use of the system,
except when the load is high and it is necessary to prioritize to
keep the system useable bt anyone.

  Being a sysadm means that you get criticism from both sides. 
If you never pay attention to your system, you can get anarchy.
Certainly the NSFnet, NEARnet, and Usenet guidelines *require* that we
pay some attention to our system, at least to the extent of stopping
commercial posts and trading of pirated software. Severe hardware
limitations and network bandwidth limitations force us to set and
enforce usage priorities; we do not have the CPU or the net capacity
to have, say, a dozen users running irc clients at the same time as a
database course assignment is due. 

  We try to stay as close to anarchy as we can and still get work
done. We carry ALL the alt groups and don't do any monitoring of them.
We allow users as much disk space as we dare (if every user used their
quota our disk would be 120% full) and when the disk fills up we send
warnings until it empties again, so that we don't have to go into
user's directories or restrict disk use by type. Since everyone
suffers when the disk fills up, users are good about deleting their
files themselves. We tolerate a high load of file transfers of
pornographic GIF's; we  limit this to non-prime-time hours, based
on the CPU and net load, but we don't forbid anything.

It certainly isn't a productive use of MY time to go chasing
users around, and I'm not interested in being a cop. I would be
willing to bet that we have one of the most liberal and productive
university labs in the country. 

Compare us to the REAL world, as well as to abstract ideals. I think
we come pretty close, and I think our users agree.






