I think what you want is something called PGP.  Now if somebody would just
explain to me whether it's legal to use it....

PGP is an encryption program ("pretty good
privacy") which among other things allows you to append 
a short coded authentication text
to *unencrypted* email messages which proves you wrote it.  

Three or four lines of garbage at the end of your email, 
which have been encrypted by your (secret) "private key"
can be decoded by anyone who has your (not secret) "public key".  
Similarly, messages designed for your eyes only can be encrypted 
with the "public key" and only the holder of the "private key" 
can read them.  

As I understand it, PGP works out a checksum from the email and 
the private key, encrypts it, and the holders of the public key 
can tell if the message if from the person it claims to be, and 
whether or not the message has been altered in any way.

Thus, the newsgroup idea is not quite as fanciful as it sounds -- which
is not the same thing as saying that it is a good idea!  (I have doubts: 
I for sure wouldn't read it.) 

But if the thing were created, it might, for
example, be moderated by a trusted party, who would do the work of
authentication for us.  (Authentication of messages from 
people you don't know can be complex, since they need to be 
introduced by someone you _do_ know.)

If nothing else, the moderator could refuse posts lacking a PGP signature.

The only problem is, there appears to be some question as to whether the
technique used in PGP is copyrighted by someone other than the author of the
program... 

I shoould also note tht the thing still lacks a technophobe-level 
front end (but apparently someone is working on it). 

The copyright uncertainty has been enough to keep me from using it.
