Unclear at the moment; its all fairly baroque. The Federal Government has 
recently proposed a Digital Signature Standard (DSS) which does exactly what
you outline for PGP, namely authenticating signatures. What it does *not*
do that PGP *does* do is also encrypt whole messages. The Government has
designed the now-famous Skipjack algorithm/clipper Chip for encryption
instead.

The Government proposes to grant an exclusive license to Public Key Partners 
for DSS, thereby creating a de facto standard. PKP holds the patents which
are relevant to DSS with the exception of one patent, the Schnorr patent.
PKP are also the people suing Zimmerman, the author of PGP, for patent 
infringement.

So much for the facts. what follows is speculation.

PGP interferes with PKP's ability to make a profit because a) they didn't write
it and b) even if they did charge a licensing fee, the patent it supposedly 
infringes on runs out in about 7 years, while the patent for DSS will be 
around for 17 years. Meanwhile, the Government wants a good digital signature
program which can't be used to encrypt whole docs, as that would interfere
with Clipper. However, despite the fact that most of the research that 
went into DSS was done with taxpayer funding, the Schnorr patent was not, and 
Schnorr is cross-licensed only with PKP.

My personal conclusion  is that PKP needs the gov't license, the gov't needs 
DSS, and PGP interferes with both goals. 

On Monday several organizations and individuals filed arguments opposing 
the proposed license as an improper creation of a de facto monopoly. There
will probably be a decision by the end of the month as to whether to grant 
the license or to reopen the public comment period for another 60 days.




