No, not at all!  I'm just trying to understand the assertions of 
those who claim that the net, in and of itself, is inhospitable to 
women/minorities/whatever.  Let me try to explain........

I tend to believe that *any* discussion of sexual/gender issues, in 
*any* forum (electronic, print, human or whatever), will bring the 
worms out of the woodwork.  I've seen it happen in public meetings 
(local government, student associations, etc.), print media (letters 
to the editor of our local paper), churches and talk radio; in fact, 
I've seen it in *every* public forum.  The electronic nature of Use-
net makes no difference in this respect; I don't believe that Usenet 
is any more (or less) prone to such incidents as any other public forum.

The flames you mentioned came after you mentioned that you are a 
lesbian; in other words, after gender/sexual issues were introduced 
into the discussion (in an admittedly minor fashion, as you said).  
I've been speaking, in email, with a person who received sexual 
flamage after responding to another gender-specific posting in a 
sci.* newsgroup.  Would not a similar incident have occured in almost 
any other public forum, given the impetus of sexual/gender commentary?

I'd still like to see an example of sexual flamage that occured in
the *absence* of sexual/gender discussions.  Have you ever received
a sexual flame "out of the blue" on a subject that didn't touch on
gender/sexuality?  I just have a *really* hard time imagining someone 
jumping into a discussion of Unix file system internals with comments 
like, "she's a *woman*; she can't possibly know what she's talking 
about.".......

I hope you can see the distinction that I'm trying to make.  I don't
intend to assert that sexual/gender flamage doesn't occur; I think
we all know otherwise.  I am, however, unconvinced that it occurs
frequently *without* the stimulus of gender/sexual commentary, such
as your (inadvertent) mention of your sexuality.  If this assertion
is correct, it seems to me that the symptoms will be the same in *any* 
public forum; if so, Usenet doesn't deserve to be singled out.

Believe it or not, I know (to a limited extent) how you feel.
I recently made a posting to soc.bi, soc.motss and soc.veterans
(I believe that was the crossposting...) that supported the
removal of the military's ban on homosexuals.  I received over
a dozen *extremely* graphic messages referring to my sexuality,
genitalia and general state of mind.  The difference seems to
be that I can simply delete the messages and forget about them.
Is it *that* difficult to ignore idiots?  After all, they are just
words on a screen; nothing I can do will change their mind (except,
perhaps, demonstrating enough maturity to avoid sinking to their
level), and they won't change mine.

--Wes

