Excerpts from netnews.comp.admin.policy: 12-Aug-93 Re: A policy draft
paraphrase Steve Simmons@iti.org (808)

> Apply this policy to a classroom.  "Please do not shout obscenities or
> attempt to drown out the lecturer in classrooms during lectures.  There
> is no sanction if you do, but we believe that such posts are disrespectful
> to others and inappropriate for a university enviornment."  Would the
> faculty approve?


Comparing a classroom with "public forums" is ridiculous.  People pay
money and attend a class at a university [presumably] to learn.  To have
this disrupted is rude to the usually -single- person who you are paying
to hear.  

Take Usenet as the classic public forum.  Usenet has places where people
are there to learn, but it is also highly recreational and is not a
lecture [or even lab] situation. It is impossible to drown out one
persons voice with another post!   To anyone who uses Usenet and whines
about what s/he sees my response is generally caveat emptor, or First
Time Feet First.   Know what you're getting into and if you don't like
it, you're free to leave.  I have yet to hear of someone saying, "my
job/life depends on Usenet."

The more I think about it the more I cannot think of a required
public-forum.  Except for, possibly, class-related local 'bboards',
which cannot really be called public, I can't think of a situation where
you must read and/or post to public forums.  

I like the draft policy, as far as what 'appropriate use' is.   In the
end, however, I feel it's too assuming on the maturity of it's users and
will need a way to deal with problems that may come up.  Sorry, but I
live in the real world where there's always some little kiddie
[emotional age not physical] who will smack around the limits of "free
speech" [ie. wanting the freedom without the responsibility].  Not to
restart an old flame war again. :)

